      "problem": "Solve for $x$ where $-\\dfrac{3}{2x}=\\dfrac{2}{x+7}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that $x=0$ and $x=-7$  cannot be solutions since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nTo solve the equation, we move the negative sign onto the numerator of the fraction and then apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t-\\dfrac{3}{2x}&=\\dfrac{2}{x+7}\\[5pt]\t\n        \\dfrac{-3}{2x}&=\\dfrac{2}{x+7}\\[5pt]\t\n\t(-3) \\cdot (x+7) &=2x \\cdot (2) \\[5pt]\t\n\t-3x - 21&=  4x\n\t}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t-3x - 21&= 4x\\[5pt]\t\n\t-3x -21 + 3x&=4x + 3x\\[5pt]\t\n\t-21&=7x\\[5pt]\t\n\t\\dfrac{-21}{7}&=\\dfrac{7x}{7} \\[5pt]\t\n\t-3 &= x\n\t}\n$$\n\nSince  $x =   - 3$ is different from $x=0$ and $x=-7$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x= -3.$",
